# @SANDBOX@

The forge VM's only way out. **GENERATED by `dr-forge gateway`** from Draugr's
`share/gateway.example/`, and meant to be regenerated rather than maintained: nothing here is
tracked by git, so there is nothing to commit, review or push.

The VM has **no network adapter**. Its proxy settings point at its own `127.0.0.1:3128`, an ssh
reverse tunnel carries that to this mound's published port `127.0.0.1:18888`, and tinyproxy here
forwards to the internet through the mound's `sbx` policy. So **the `sbx` policy is the allowlist**,
managed with the same tools as any mound: `dr-policy`, kits.

```text
VM (no adapter) -> guest 127.0.0.1:3128 -> ssh -R over AppSandbox's relay -> Windows 127.0.0.1:18888
  -> this mound: tinyproxy, allow-all -> sbx policy (defaults - .draugr/kit denies + the mixins' allows) -> internet
```

The design, the boundary it keeps and the acceptance criteria are in Draugr's
`docs/FORGE.md`.

## What is where

| | |
|---|---|
| `.draugr.conf` | a `shell` mound, 2 GiB, 2 CPUs, ports `18888` → 8888 and `18445` → 445, three kits, the two mounted directories |
| `.draugr/kit/` | installs tinyproxy and samba; `deny:` narrows the `sbx` defaults. Allows **nothing**: every allow lives in a mixin |
| `kits/unreal/` | every host the Epic Launcher, Fab, the Unreal Editor and Visual Studio were seen to need |
| `kits/base/` | hosts any forge VM needs whatever it is built for: the pinned MSYS2 repository, PyPI and `uv`, AutoHotkey, VS Code, Microsoft-account sign-in |
| `.draugr/hooks/post-up` | the keeper session, tinyproxy, the vault's SMB server, and a probe |

## Bringing it up

You do not, normally. `dr-forge up` in the game project brings this mound up first through
`DRAUGR_REQUIRES`, then the VM, then the tunnel. From here, `dr-up` starts this mound on its own —
useful when you are changing the allowlist and nothing else.

**Exactly one gateway can run.** The ports above are fixed, `18022` is pinned, and the tunnel
supervisor holds one mutex, so a second gateway — generated or hand-made — must not be started
beside the first. On Windows that fails *quietly*: a second process may bind a port that is already
bound, and the two then take turns answering.

## Why tinyproxy filters nothing

It has no `Allow` lines and no destination rules, and that is deliberate twice over. The `sbx`
policy is the allowlist, so a second list here would be a second place to keep in step — and every
client arrives from the `sbx` forwarder at `172.17.0.10`, so `Allow` could not tell one from another
anyway.

## Adding a host

Put it in the **mixin it belongs to**, not in `.draugr/kit`: `kits/unreal/` for an Unreal or
Visual Studio host, `kits/base/` for something any Windows VM would need. Then `dr-up --recreate`,
because applying a kit rebuilds the mound.

`dr-policy --allow <host>` opens it on the *running* mound, which is machine state rather than repo
state: it is the right tool for finding out what a tool wants, and the wrong one for keeping it.
`dr-policy --denied` is what to read when something in the guest fails for no visible reason.

**`deny` beats `allow` everywhere.** A host listed in `.draugr/kit`'s deny list cannot be opened by
any mixin or any `--allow`; delete its line there first. And never deny `**`.

## The vault, and the scratch directory

The two directories `DRAUGR_MOUNTS` brings into this mound are served to the guest over SMB on
`127.0.0.1:18445`:

- the **vault holder**, mounted `:ro`, so the kernel refuses a write however Samba is configured.
  The VM must not be able to write here — it is where read-only things the VM needs sit beside the
  Fab cache.
- one **scratch** directory the guest may write, which is the only writable path across the boundary.

The share needs a password, and Draugr neither writes nor reads it: put a random string in
`~/.ssh/draugr_vault_smb.pass` on the host. That file is often the only record of a password the
guest also stores, so nothing in Draugr touches it. Without it, `post-up` says so and serves no
share — the proxy is unaffected either way.

**What generation does not do for you:** granting the mound's user read access to those two host
directories. That is host-side ACL work on paths only you know, and it is still the hand-made
gateway's job — `docs/FORGE.md` lists *equipped* among the things `dr-forge init` has yet to take
over. A generated gateway proxies correctly without it; the share is what waits.

## Which client honours which proxy setting

The guest needs **three** settings in place — the per-user WinINet one, `netsh winhttp`, and the
environment variables — because they are separate stores and no client reads all of them. Measured:
`netsh winhttp` serves Windows' own services, installers and Windows Update, but *not* per-user
clients; .NET and PowerShell 5.1's `Invoke-WebRequest` read the per-user WinINet setting and ignore
`netsh winhttp`; `curl.exe` takes `-x` or `HTTPS_PROXY`. The Epic Launcher and the Unreal Editor do
honour the Windows proxy — both log `HttpProxyAddress = '127.0.0.1:3128'` — but which of the three
they read was not isolated, because all three were set. `winget` is the one that stayed broken, and
for its own reason: `0x8a15000f` with nothing further denied.

## Trust

`.draugr.conf` is sourced and `post-up` runs on your machine as you, so Draugr records a hash of
each and refuses a version it has not seen. Generation granted that for the two files it wrote. If
you edit either, run `dr-trust` on it — until you do, `dr-up` refuses the config and skips the hook.
